---
title: Mayonnaise
description: Lesson 4.3. A stable emulsion, and how to mend a broken one.
servings: 6
time: 15 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, eggs-and-emulsions]
lesson: "4.3"
technique: emulsifying
status: draft
---

> Technique: a stable emulsion. Mayonnaise is mostly oil, held as tiny
> droplets in a little water by the egg yolk. Add the oil slowly enough
> and it thickens; add it too fast and it breaks.

In a #bowl{} set on a damp cloth so it cannot slide, whisk @egg yolk{1} with @Dijon mustard{1%tsp}, @lemon juice{1%tbsp} and @salt{1/4%tsp}.

> Science: mayonnaise is an oil-in-water emulsion, 65 to 80% fat by
> weight: the oil is the droplets and the lemon juice and yolk are the
> water they hang in. Egg yolk contains lecithin, an emulsifier that
> surrounds each oil droplet and keeps it from touching the next.
> Mustard adds a second emulsifier. Look up: oil-in-water emulsion,
> lecithin, phospholipid, emulsifier.
-- source: Lan Lam, "Homemade Mayo with Pasteurized Yolks", America's Test Kitchen, 2023-02-28, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/2106-homemade-mayo-that-keeps ; McClements, "Critical review of techniques and methodologies for characterization of emulsion stability", Critical Reviews in Food Science and Nutrition 47(7):611-649, 2007, https://doi.org/10.1080/10408390701289292 ; composition from the introduction of an egg-free mayonnaise study, ACS Omega, 2026, https://doi.org/10.1021/acsomega.6c00909

Measure @oil{150%ml}(sunflower or rapeseed) into a jug. Whisking all the time, add the oil a few drops at a time until the mixture thickens, then in a thin stream, until it is all in and the mayonnaise holds its shape.

> Why slowly: adding the oil too quickly is one of the most common
> causes of failure. The droplets find each other and merge before the
> yolk can coat them, and the mixture turns thin and greasy.

> If it goes wrong: put 1 tablespoon of water in a clean bowl. Whisking
> hard, add the broken mixture to it a little at a time, as slowly as
> you added the oil. It will come back, a little looser than before.
-- source: "How to Fix Failed Mayo", Cook's Illustrated, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/cooksillustrated/how_tos/13363-how-to-fix-broken-mayo

Taste, and add more lemon juice or salt if it needs it. Keep it in the fridge and eat it within 2 days.

> Safety: this recipe uses a raw egg yolk, and fresh eggs can carry
> Salmonella. For dishes with raw or undercooked eggs the US FDA says
> to use pasteurised eggs or egg products. You can also heat the yolk
> yourself, whisked with 3 tablespoons of water, to 71 °C before you
> start. This matters most for children, older adults and pregnant
> women.
-- source: US Food and Drug Administration, "What You Need to Know About Egg Safety", content current as of 2024-03-05, https://www.fda.gov/food/buy-store-serve-safe-food/what-you-need-know-about-egg-safety ; Lan Lam (as above, "heat the liquid eggs to 160 degrees") ; Food Standards Scotland, "Food storage", https://www.foodstandards.gov.scot/consumers/food-safety/at-home/rice (dishes containing eggs: keep in the fridge and eat within 2 days)

> Next time, without the recipe: one yolk, a spoonful each of mustard
> and acid, and oil added so slowly it bores you. If it breaks, start
> again from a spoonful of water.
